{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // Playwright transpiles the specs without type-checking them, so this file
    // is only worth anything if `tsc -p e2e/tsconfig.json` actually runs clean —
    // otherwise a real error in a spec is indistinguishable from the noise and
    // nobody looks. Measured in this tree, at target ES2020 without
    // skipLibCheck: **100 errors, 96 of them in playwright-core's own .d.ts and
    // 4 in tests/**. Those four were real and had been sitting there unread,
    // which is the whole argument for this change.
    //
    // Count the whole output of a `-p` run. Passing file arguments makes tsc
    // ignore this file entirely and resolve libs differently — a different
    // program, not a shorter run of the same one — and piping through `head`
    // reports the lines that fitted rather than the errors that exist. Both
    // have produced a wrong number in a comment like this one before.
    //
    // The noise is entirely playwright-core's own types: they reference Node
    // globals (Buffer, fs, stream, child_process) that need @types/node, and
    // Symbol.asyncDispose, which needs a lib above ES2020. skipLibCheck drops
    // the first set, ES2022 the second — neither weakens checking of tests/**,
    // which was verified by breaking a spec on purpose and watching tsc catch
    // it, not by reading the flag's documentation.
    //
    // @types/node is deliberately not a dependency, as in MyMail and MyNotes:
    // it would be a second entry in a lockfile whose being single-dependency is
    // the reason it is reviewable. MyCal is the one of the three whose suite
    // actually reached for Node globals — `path`, `__dirname` and `Buffer`,
    // three of the four errors above — so keeping that rule cost something
    // here. See the comments in tests/import.spec.ts for what replaced them.
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["tests/**/*.ts"]
}
